{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Distributing content across Regions","context":"# Distributing content across Regions\n\nYou can set up two AWS Elemental MediaConnect flows to distribute content from one AWS Region\nto another. In this scenario, you create one flow in the Region that is closest to your\ncontribution encoder and a second flow in the Region that is closest to your receiver.\nThe following illustration shows this process.\n\nThis topic assumes that you already know how to create a\nflow and add outputs to a flow.\n\n###### To distribute content across Regions (console)\n\n1. In the AWS Region that is closest to your source, create a flow. (We'll\n refer to this as flow A.)\n2. Review the **Details** page for flow A to\n determine its egress IP address.\n3. In the AWS Region that is closest to your destination, create a second flow\n (flow B) with the following details:\n\n * Source type: Choose **Standard\n source**.\n * Protocol: Choose **Zixi\n push**.\n * Inbound\n port:\n Selecting\n Zixi push as the protocol will automatically set this port to\n `2088`.\n * Allowlist\n CIDR block: Enter a CIDR value that includes the egress IP of flow\n A.\n4. Review the **Details** page, **Source** tab for flow B to determine its ingest IP\n address.\n5. In flow A, create an output with the following details:\n\n * Protocol: Choose **Zixi push**.\n * IP address: Enter the ingest IP address of flow B.\n * Port: Enter `2088`","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/mediaconnect/latest/ug/distribution-across-regions.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Troubleshooting SRT and Zixi with AWS Elemental MediaConnect | AWS for M&E Blog","context":"### Contribution best practices\n\nHigh availability contribution design requires redundancy. Two encoders output locked and egressing out different network circuits, carried over different providers, will provide both encoder and network diversity. These streams should be going to separate MediaConnect flows in separate Availability Zones (AZ). This basic architecture principle can achieve five 9’s of availability.\n\nDiagram 1 shows a typical resilient and redundant streaming media workflow consisting of two encoders, two separate network providers (Direct Connect), two MediaConnect receivers, and a standard channel AWS Elemental MediaLive channel that runs in two AZs.\n\n[](https://d2908q01vomqb2.cloudfront.net/fb644351560d8296fe6da332236b1f8d61b2828a/2025/02/11/SRT-and-Zixi-Contribution-Reliable-Transmission.png)\n\n*Diagram 1: A typical streaming media workflow.*\n\nIt’s recommended to use AWS Direct Connect when possible. AWS Direct Connect provides a consistent network experience, with lower latencies and higher throughput rates compared to internet-based connections. If you have access to Direct Connect links you *must* enable VPC endpoints on your MediaConnect flows to confirm that no traffic egresses through Internet Gateways (IGW).\n\nIf you must send video through the public internet, just know your traffic is routed through multiple networks and service providers, increasing the latency and potential for packet loss. While Direct Connect and virtual private clouds (VPCs) are *more* reliable than the public internet, your feeds are not totally immune to issues in this configuration","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/media/troubleshooting-srt-and-zixi-with-aws-elemental-mediaconnect/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"How to Set Up a Resilient End-to-End Live Workflow Using AWS Elemental Products and Services: Part 3 | AWS Media Blog","context":"### *Multi-regions for event-based streaming*\n\nThis scenario aims to provide the same level of resilience as the architecture described above, but with lower egress bandwidth requirements at the event site, and shares the same downstream workflow, starting from MediaLive. Using MediaConnect’s flow cloning feature, we can reach the same level of availability for the contribution stream as the architecture described above by sending only four mezzanine streams instead of eight. Each pair of contribution encoders uses a MediaConnect flow as ingest target, and each flow sends a copy of the stream to another MediaConnect flow in the second AWS Region, so that all four contribution streams are available as sources for MediaLive in each Region. This enables you to switch between two input groups for each MediaLive deployment: If one MediaLive deployment becomes unavailable, the other will access all of the four contribution streams and ensure the availability of the intermediary ABR streams in HLS. MediaPackage’s auto-scaling architecture will provide the necessary level of resilience level in each region, guaranteeing availability of the streams exposed to end users.\n\n[](https://d2908q01vomqb2.cloudfront.net/fb644351560d8296fe6da332236b1f8d61b2828a/2019/03/20/scenario_3_2a.png)\n\nThis final reference architecture concludes our journey through resilient live workflows options. In a future blog post, we will explain how to set up an end-to-end monitoring system using the Media Services Application Mapper (MSAM) solution, which allows users to bring AWS Elemental on-premises encoders, AWS Elemental Media Services, and other related AWS services together in a single monitoring view.\n\nView Comments","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/media/part3-how-to-set-up-a-resilient-end-to-end-live-workflow-using-aws-elemental-products-and-services/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Announces Elemental MediaConnect Router - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router, a new capability from Amazon Web Services (AWS), is now generally available. This service enables broadcasters and content providers to dynamically route live video between sources and destinations within the AWS network. MediaConnect Router supports complex scenarios such as switching between primary and backup feeds, routing regional variants independently, and managing multiple feeds for comprehensive coverage. It optimizes content delivery across the AWS network, reducing transport latency and improving packet delivery reliability compared to standard transport technologies. MediaConnect Router is a fully managed capability that supports routing between inputs and outputs in any supported region and between private and public endpoints. It can be used independently or alongside existing MediaConnect flows and is part of a larger video workflow with AWS Elemental. The service is available in all standard AWS Regions. MediaConnect Router can be accessed through the MediaConnect console, API, or AWS CDK. It eliminates operational overhead and unused capacity costs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/elemental-mediaconnect-router/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router now supports endpoint connection management - AWS","context":"AWS Elemental MediaConnect Router has introduced a new endpoint registry feature that enables managing external connections in a centralized manner. The registry supports both standard endpoints, such as cameras and software, and connections to other AWS accounts. Cross-account sharing of registered endpoints streamlines live video workflows, reduces costs, and decreases latency. The endpoint registry offers a single interface for managing all external connections to MediaConnect, providing features like AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM) for cross-account sharing, encryption and protocol controls verification, and built-in connection management with Connection Manager. The endpoint registry is available in all AWS Regions where MediaConnect Router is available, at no additional cost. Use cases include content distributors, broadcasters, and media workflows requiring secure cross-account video transport","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/mediaconnect-cross-account-endpoints/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-media-services","skill_description":"Designs, deploys, reviews, evaluates, audits, and troubleshoots video streaming workflows across AWS media services (MediaConnect, MediaLive, MediaConvert, MediaPackage v2, MediaTailor, CloudFront). Includes per-service validation rules, cross-service workflow patterns for live streaming, linear channels, VOD pipelines, and live-to-VOD, service-specific troubleshooting, best practices assessment, cost optimization, encoder settings, DRM, SCTE-35 ad insertion, epoch locking, and CDN cache configuration.\n\nServices: mediaconnect, medialive, mediaconvert, mediapackage, mediatailor, cloudfront\nTasks: review, deploy, troubleshoot, audit, optimize\nPersona: video-engineer, solutions-architect\nWorkload: live-streaming, vod, linear-channel","skill_name":"aws-media-services"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-messaging-and-streaming","skill_description":"Guides general use of AWS messaging and streaming services. Covers Amazon SQS, Amazon SNS, Amazon EventBridge, Amazon MQ, Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, Amazon Data Firehose, Amazon Managed Service for Apache Flink, and Amazon Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka (MSK).  Use when reasoning about messaging and streaming patterns. Also identifies which AWS service owns each customer communication channel: email (Amazon SES), and WhatsApp, SMS, MMS, RCS, voice and mobile push (the AWS End User Messaging family of services). Routes the request to the specialized skill for that channel. Defers to the channel's specialized skill when the user already named a specific channel. In general, use specific skills or documentation searches for detailed service-specific questions. Do NOT use for MSK or Managed Service for Apache Flink questions, prefer specific skills. Does not configure customer communication channels; defers to specific skills.\n\n\nServices: sqs, sns, eventbridge, mq, kinesis, kds, firehose, flink, msf, msk, kafka, ses, socialmessaging, pinpoint-sms-voice-v2\nTasks: learn, architect, debug, deploy\nPersona: developer, architect, devops\nWorkload: serverless, event-driven, streaming, data-analytics, messaging\n*","skill_name":"aws-messaging-and-streaming"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"arc-region-switch","skill_description":"Answers questions about Amazon Application Recovery Controller (ARC) Region switch including architecture, plans, execution blocks, workflows, triggers, active/active vs active/passive, cross-account support, recovery time, dashboards, and customer positioning. Applicable when users ask about ARC Region switch adoption, design, or troubleshooting.\n\nServices: arc-region-switch\nTasks: learn, architect, build, debug, migrate, advise\nPersona: developer, devops, solutions-architect\nWorkload: disaster-recovery, multi-region\n*","skill_name":"arc-region-switch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS MediaConnect best practice docs on region placement for flows."}

